          %h2#idea_and_definition Idea and definition
          %p
            Classically, a ‘Schur functor’ is a specific sort of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functor"} functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi FinDimVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S \,\colon\, FinDimVect \longrightarrow FinDimVect
          %p
            where
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            is the category of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite+dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite dimensional vector spaces
            over the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi S
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S(X)
            is obtained by taking the
            = succeed "th" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+power"} tensor power
            of the vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            and then picking out a subspace that transforms in a certain way with respect to the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+group"} symmetric group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories"} categories
            resembling
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/categories+of+representations"} categories of representations
            of a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundles"} vector bundles
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/natural+transformation"} naturally
            = succeed "\u2019" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformation"} pseudonaturally
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+categories"} Cauchy complete categories
          %p The most famous examples of Schur functors are these:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi n
                        %mi th
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+power"} symmetric power
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↦
                      %msup
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto S^n(X)
                is a Schur functor.
            %li
              %p
                For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi n
                        %mi th
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/alternating+power"} alternating power
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ↦
                      %msup
                        %mi Λ
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto \Lambda^n(X)
                is a Schur functor.
          %p
            More generally, complex
            = succeed "s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/irreducible+representation"} irreducible representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            correspond to
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young+diagram"} Young diagrams
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            as a sum of natural numbers listed in decreasing order. For example, this 17-box Young diagram:
          #Young{:style => "text-align:center"}
            %svg{:height => "140", "se:nonce" => "39384", :width => "120", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/2000/svg", "xmlns:se" => "http://svg-edit.googlecode.com", "xmlns:xlink" => "http://www.w3.org/1999/xlink"}
              / Created with SVG-edit - http://svg-edit.googlecode.com/
              %desc Young diagram (5,4,4,2,1,1)
              %g
                %title Layer 1
                %rect#svg_39384_1{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "10"}
                %rect#svg_39384_2{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "10"}
                %rect#svg_39384_3{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "50", :y => "10"}
                %rect#svg_39384_4{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "70", :y => "10"}
                %rect#svg_39384_5{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "90", :y => "10"}
                %rect#svg_39384_6{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "30"}
                %rect#svg_39384_7{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "30"}
                %rect#svg_39384_8{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "50", :y => "30"}
                %rect#svg_39384_9{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "70", :y => "30"}
                %rect#svg_39384_10{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "50"}
                %rect#svg_39384_11{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "50"}
                %rect#svg_39384_12{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "50", :y => "50"}
                %rect#svg_39384_13{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "70", :y => "50"}
                %rect#svg_39384_14{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "70"}
                %rect#svg_39384_15{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "30", :y => "70"}
                %rect#svg_39384_16{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "90"}
                %rect#svg_39384_17{:fill => "#ffdddd", :height => "20", :stroke => "#000000", "stroke-width" => "2", :width => "20", :x => "10", :y => "110"}
          %p
            describes the partition of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 17
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 17
            as
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 5
                    %mo +
                    %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mn 4
                    %mo +
                    %mn 2
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                    %mo +
                    %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 5 + 4 + 4 + 2 + 1 + 1
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mn 17
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{17}
          %p
            In general, here is the recipe for constructing the Schur functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda
            associated to an
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed "):" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Given an
                = succeed "-box" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
                and a vector space
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi X
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+power"} tensor power
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi X
                          %mrow
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
            %li
              %p
                Then we pick out the subspace of
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
                consisting of tensors that are
                %em unchanged
                by any permutation that interchanges two boxes in the same
                = succeed "." do
                  %em row
            %li
              %p
                Then we project this subspace onto the space of tensors that
                %em change sign
                under any permutation that interchanges two boxes in the same
                = succeed "." do
                  %em column
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi λ
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mi X
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X)
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda
                is the Schur functor corresponding to
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi λ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
          %p It is easy to see from this description that:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The tall skinny Young diagrams with one column and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                rows give the Schur functors
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi Λ
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Lambda^n
            %li
              %p
                The short fat Young diagrams with one row and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
                columns give the Schur functors
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S^n
          %p
            We can also think of this relation between Young diagrams and Schur functors in a slightly more abstract way using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
            of the symmetric group,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            is an
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi λ
                      %mi S
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^S_\lambda \in \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msubsup
                      %mi p
                      %mi λ
                      %mi A
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^A_\lambda \in \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi A
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi A
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi S
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p^A_\lambda)^2 = p^A_\lambda \, , \; \; (p^S_\lambda)^2 = p^S_\lambda
          %p
            Now, it is easy to see that the product of commuting idempotents is idempotent. The elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^S_\lambda
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^A_\lambda
            do not commute, but amazingly, their product
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %mo =
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi A
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mi S
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda = p^A_\lambda p^S_\lambda
          %p is idempotent up to a scalar multiple!
          %p
            This element
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda \in \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            is called the
            %strong Young symmetrizer
            corresponding to the
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong Schur functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi FinDimVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda \colon FinDimVect \to FinDimVect
          %p
            defined on any finite-dimensional vector space
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            as follows:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X) = p_\lambda X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            Here we are using the fact that
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
            acts as an idempotent operator on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X)
            as defined above is the range of this operator.
          %p An even deeper approach to Schur functors is based on the relation between Young diagrams and representations of the symmetric groups. The subspace
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊆
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda = p_\lambda \mathbb{C}[S_n] \subseteq \mathbb{C}[S_n]
          %p
            has an obvious right action of the algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            is isomorphic to one of this form. Even better, this recipe sets up a one-to-one correspondence between
            = succeed "-box" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
          %p
            (Strictly speaking, to think of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
            as an element of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}[S_n]
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo ,
                    %mi …
                    %mo ,
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1, \dots, n
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Young tableau
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda
          %p
            This description of irreducible representations of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            paves the way towards an important generalization of Schur functors. First, note that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n} \cong \mathbb{C}[S_n] \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} X^{\otimes n}
          %p It follows that
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi λ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mi λ
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi p
                            %mi λ
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %mi ℂ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mi λ
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %mi ℂ
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                              %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{ S_\lambda(X) &amp;=&amp; p_\lambda X^{\otimes n} \\
                  \&amp;\cong&amp; p_\lambda \mathbb{C}[S_n] \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} X^{\otimes n} \\
                  \&amp;\cong&amp; V_\lambda \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} X^{\otimes n}
                  }
          %p
            These isomorphisms here are natural, so there is no harm in
            %em defining
            the Schur functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda
            by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(-) = V_\lambda \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} (-)^{\otimes n}
          %p This formula defines the Schur functor not only on objects but also on morphisms. Even better, we can use the same formula to define a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi FinDimVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R \colon FinDimVect \to FinDimVect
          %p
            for
            %em any
            representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi R
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R(-) = R \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} (-)^{\otimes n} \, .
          %p These more general functors are still called ‘Schur functors’.
          %p
            Even more generally, any finite direct sum of the Schur functors just described may also be called a Schur functor. In other words, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \{R_n\}_{n \ge 0}
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_n
            is a finite-dimensional representation of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %strong Schur functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R(-) = \bigoplus_{n \ge 0} R_n \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} (-)^{\otimes n} \, .
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                For each
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                        %mo ≥
                        %mn 0
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \geq 0
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi n
                        %mi th
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+power"} tensor power
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ↦
                      %msup
                        %mi V
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto V^{\otimes n}
                is a Schur functor.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                are Schur functors, the functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊕
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto F(V) \oplus G(V)
                is a Schur functor.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                are Schur functors, the functor
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto F(V) \otimes G(V)
                is also a Schur functor.
            %li
              %p
                If
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
                and
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
                are Schur functors, the composite
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                      %mo ↦
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi V
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \mapsto F(G(V))
                is a Schur functor. This way of constructing Schur functors is known as
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plethysm"} plethysm
          %h2#the_category_of_schur_functors The category of Schur functors
          %p
            There is a category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            with
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Schur functors
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi R
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi FinDimVect
                      %mo →
                      %mi FinDimVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R \colon FinDimVect \to FinDimVect
                as objects. Here
              .maruku-equation
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %munder
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo ≥
                          %mn 0
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi n
                      %msub
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mrow
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %msup
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R(-) = \bigoplus_{n \ge 0} R_n \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} (-)^{\otimes n} \,
              %p
                each
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R_n
                is a finite-dimensional representation of
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %li
              %p natural transformations between such functors as morphisms.
          %p In the rest of this article, we would like to give a conceptual explanation of this category.
          %p
            As a warm-up, let us note that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Schur
            has has a nice description in terms of groupoid of finite sets and bijections. This groupoid is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/core"} core
            of the category
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinSet"} FinSet
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi core
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi FinSet
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} core(FinSet)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            for various
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %em
              direct sum of representations of symmetric groups
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
              for various
              = succeed "?" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            It is nothing but a representation of the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/permutation+groupoid"} permutation groupoid
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨆
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P} = \bigsqcup_{n \ge 0} S_n \, ,
          %p
            where objects are natural numbers, all morphisms are automorphisms, and the automorphisms of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            form the group
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R : \mathbb{P} \to Vect
          %p
            But conceptually, the importance of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            is that it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
            of the groupoid of finite sets and bijections. So,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P} \simeq core(FinSet) \, .
          %p As a result, any Schur functor gives a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R : core(FinSet) \to Vect
          %p
            Following
            = succeed "\u2018s" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andre+Joyal"} Joyal
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} species
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/structure+type"} structure types
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                  %mo :
                  %mi core
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi FinSet
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T : core(FinSet) \to Set
          %p
            assigns to any finite set a
            %em set
            of structures of some type, a
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %em vector space
            of structures of some type, Thanks to the ‘free vector space on a set’ functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: Set \to Vect \, ,
          %p
            we can linearize any ordinary species
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi T
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} T
            and obtain a linear species
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi T
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \circ T
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Aguiar"} Marcelo Aguiar
            and
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Swapneel+Mahajan"} Swapneel Mahajan
          %ul
            %li#AMbook
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Aguiar"} Marcelo Aguiar
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Swapneel+Mahajan"} Swapneel Mahajan
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Monoidal+Functors%2C+Species+and+Hopf+Algebras"} Monoidal Functors, Species and Hopf Algebras
          %p
            However, not all
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %em finite
            direct sums of irreducible representations of permutation groups. So,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            is equivalent to the category where:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                objects are
                = succeed ":" do
                  %strong polynomial species
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo :
                      %mi core
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi Fin
                      %mo{:lspace => "0em", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} Set
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo →
                      %mi FinDimVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R: core(Fin\Set) \to FinDimVect
                such that
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                      %mn 0
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R(n) = \{0\}
                for all sufficiently large finite sets
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi n
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %li
              %p morphisms are natural transformations.
          %p
            We call this the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong category of polynomial species
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi X
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %msub
                    %mi R
                    %mi n
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R(X) = \bigoplus_{n \ge 0} R_n \otimes_{\mathbb{C}[S_n]} X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            gives rise to a formal power series called its
            = succeed ":" do
              %strong generating function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi dim
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi R
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi n
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{n \ge 0} \frac{dim(R_n) \, x^n}{n!}  \, ,
          %p
            and this power series is a polynomial precisely when
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is a polynomial species.
          %p
            The category of representations of any groupoid has many nice features. For example, it is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/abelian+category"} abelian category
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            is equivalent to the category of polynomial species, it too is symmetric monoidal abelian.
          %p
            In particular,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            has two monoidal structures,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊗
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊕
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F \oplus G)(V) = F(V) \oplus G(V)
          %p and
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi V
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F \otimes G)(V) = F(V) \otimes G(V)
          %p
            Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            distributes over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊕
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
          %p
            In the literature on species, the operation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊕
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \oplus
            is often called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong addition
            %a{:href => "#AMbook"} Aguiar and Mahajan
            call
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            the
            %em Hadamard product
            (see section 8.1.2).
          %p
            But the category of representations of a groupoid has even more nice features when the groupoid itself has a
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal structure
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            has, in fact,
            %em two
            important monoidal structures, coming from the product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            and disjoint union
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            of finite sets. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            distributes over
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            into a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/rig+category"} rig category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            two more monoidal structures, making it into a rig category in another way. The same is true for the subcategory
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Schur
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
          %p
            In the literature on species, the monoidal structure coming from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} +
            is often called
            = succeed "," do
              %strong multiplication
            = succeed "." do
              %em Cauchy product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ×
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \times
            has no commonly used name, but it deserves to be called the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong Dirichlet product
          %p
            Aguiar and Mahajan point out that some of the relationships between the “pointwise” products (addition and Hadamard) and the “Day” products (Cauchy and Dirichlet) can be described in terms of
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/duoidal+categories"} duoidal categories
          %p
            On top of all this, the composite of Schur functors is again a Schur functor. This gives
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            a fifth monoidal structure: the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong plethystic tensor product
          %p
            Mathematicians often work with a decategorified version of
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Schur
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grothendieck+group"} Grothendieck group
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+functions"} symmetric functions
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            possesses endow this ring with corresponding structures. Among other things, it is the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lambda-ring"} lambda-ring
            on one generator. As we shall see, this corresponds to the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            is the free
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear category
            on one object.
          %h2#schur_functors_on_more_general_categories Schur functors on more general categories
          %p We have described Schur functors as special functors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi FinDimVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \,\colon\, FinDimVect \to FinDimVect
          %p
            But in fact, functors such as the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
            alternating power,
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
            symmetric power, etc. make sense in much wider contexts. For starters, we can replace the complex numbers by any field
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            of characteristic zero, and everything in our discussion still works. More importantly, Schur functors can be applied to any
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cauchy+complete+category"} Cauchy complete
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+category"} linear category
            (“
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+category"}> tensor category
            ”). Here by
            %strong linear category
            we mean a category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/enriched+category"} enriched
            over
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %strong Cauchy complete
            when:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                it has
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproducts"} biproducts
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sums"} direct sums
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotents
                = succeed "." do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/split+idempotent"} split
          %p To illustrate the full breadth of this generalization, here are a few examples:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                the category
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Vect"} Vect
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/characteristic+zero"} characteristic zero
            %li
              %p
                the category
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+representations"} category of representations
                of any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
                on vector spaces (or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/finite-dimensional+vector+spaces"} finite-dimensional vector spaces
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                the category of
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+vector+spaces"} super vector spaces
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/graded+vector+space"} graded vector spaces
                or
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/chain+complexes"} chain complexes
                over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                for
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mi ℝ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k = \mathbb{R}
                or
                = succeed "," do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vector+bundle"} vector bundles
                over any
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/topological+space"} topological space
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/manifold"} manifold
            %li
              %p
                the category of algebraic vector bundles over any
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+variety"} algebraic variety
                (or more generally,
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scheme"} scheme
                or
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/algebraic+stack"} algebraic stack
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %li
              %p
                the category of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coherent+sheaves"} coherent sheaves
                of vector spaces over any algebraic variety (or scheme or algebraic stack) over
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
          %p These examples can be hybridized, and thus they multiply indefinitely: for example, we could take coherent sheaves of chain complexes, or vector bundles equipped with a group action, and so on.
          %p
            In the following subsections, we explain how to define Schur functors on any category of this sort. A somewhat novel feature of our treatment is that we
            %em do not require the theory of Young diagrams
            to define and study Schur functors.
          %p
            Our strategy is as follows. We fix a symmetric monoidal Cauchy complete linear category,
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            begins life as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in the symmetric monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            by a “change of base” functor going from
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
            to construct idempotents on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V^{\otimes k}
            for any object
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \in C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda : C \to C
          %h3#change_of_base Change of base
          %p
            To achieve the desired change of base, let
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat
            be the linear category whose objects are integers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ≥
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq 0
            and whose morphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo →
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \to n
            are
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                  %mo ×
                  %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \times n
            matrices with entries in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is Cauchy complete and in particular has finite biproducts (direct sums), there is an evident linear functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mat
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat \to C
          %p
            which takes
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi I
                      %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^m
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            copies of the tensor unit
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi FinDimVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = FinDimVect
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mat
                  %mo →
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat \to FinDimVect,
          %p
            taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Mat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Mat
            as a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeleton"} skeleton
            of
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon FinDimVect \to C
          %p
            which, up to unique linear isomorphism, is the unique linear functor taking
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
            of this form must take the tensor unit
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            to
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            (up to coherent isomorphism, as always), and in fact
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            is symmetric monoidal, because there is a canonical isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi m
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mi n
                  %mo ≅
                  %msup
                    %mi I
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mi n
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I^m \otimes I^n \cong I^{m n},
          %p
            using the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            preserves direct sums in each argument, and the fact that there is a canonical isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi I
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi I
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I \otimes I \cong I
          %p In summary, we have the following proposition.
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition Proposition
            %p
              There is exactly one symmetric monoidal linear functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                      %mi FinDimVect
                      %mo →
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon FinDimVect \to C
          %h3#the_action_of_young_symmetrizers The action of Young symmetrizers
          %p
            Next we explain how given an object
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \in C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            acts as an idempotent on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            For this we only need to know a little bit about the group algebra
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maschke%27s+theorem"} Maschke's theorem
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[G]
            decomposes as a direct sum of matrix algebras
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi λ
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \bigoplus_{\lambda} hom(V_\lambda, V_\lambda)
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
            ranges over isomorphism classes of irreducible representations of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(V_\lambda, V_\lambda)
            thus correspond to certain special elements
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi λ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda \in k[G]
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent"} idempotent
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msubsup
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                    %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda^2 = p_\lambda \, .
          %p
            We are particularly interested in the case
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G = S_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
            are ‘Young symmetrizers’. However, we will not need the formula for these idempotents.
          %p
            The key step is to apply base change to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n] = \bigoplus_{\sigma \in S_n}  k
          %p
            is a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid"} monoid
            in the monoidal category
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mi FinDimVect
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon FinDimVect \to C
            is a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+functor"} monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            carries
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            to a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoid+object"} monoid object
            in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            by abuse of notation. As an object of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          #eq:sum.maruku-equation
            %span.maruku-eq-number (1)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n] \cong \bigoplus_{\sigma \in S_n}  I
          %p
            There is a general concept of what it means for a monoid in a monoidal category to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/action"} act
            on an object in that category. In particular, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            is an object of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            acts on the tensor power
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S_n
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi σ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma : X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            Putting these together with the help of
            %a.maruku-eqref{:href => "#eq:sum"} (1)
            we obtain a morphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n] \otimes X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
          %p which is the desired action.
          %p
            Finally, we would like to describe how each Young symmetrizer
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda \in k[S_n]
            acts on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x \in k[S_n]
            gives a linear map from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
            to this, we obtain a morphism which by abuse of language we call
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi I
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x : I \to k[S_n]
          %p This then yields an endomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo ˜
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{x}: X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n }
          %p given as the composite
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mo ≅
                  %mi I
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %mo ⟶
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mn 1
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo ⟶
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n} \stackrel{\cong}{\longrightarrow} I \otimes X^{\otimes n} \stackrel{x \otimes 1}{\longrightarrow} k[S_n] \otimes X^{\otimes n} \longrightarrow X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            It is easy to check that for any
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo ,
                    %mi y
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x,y \in k[S_n]
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mi y
                    %mo ˜
                  %mo =
                  %mover
                    %mi x
                    %mo ˜
                  %mover
                    %mi y
                    %mo ˜
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{x y} = \widetilde{x} \widetilde{y}
          %p
            Thus for any Young symmetrizer
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi p
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi p
                      %mo ˜
                    %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{p}_\lambda : X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            is idempotent, because
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p_\lambda
            is.
          %h3#constructing_schur_functors Constructing Schur functors
          %p By construction, the morphisms
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi p
                      %mo ˜
                    %mi λ
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widetilde{p}_\lambda : X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
          %p
            are the components of a natural transformation from the functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \mapsto X^{\otimes n}
            to itself. Since idempotents split in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mn 1
                    %mo −
                    %msub
                      %mover
                        %mi p
                        %mo ˜
                      %mi λ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 - \widetilde{p}_\lambda
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                  %mover
                    %munder
                      %mo ⇉
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi p
                          %mo ˜
                        %mi λ
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n} \stackrel{\widetilde{p}_\lambda}{\underset{1}{\rightrightarrows}} X^{\otimes n}
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition Definition
            %p
              For any Young diagram
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \lambda
              %strong Schur functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda: C \to C
              is defined as follows. Given an object
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X)
              be the cokernel of
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mn 1
                      %mo −
                      %msub
                        %mover
                          %mi p
                          %mo ˜
                        %mi λ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (1 - \widetilde{p}_\lambda) : X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi X
                    %mo →
                    %mi Y
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: X \to Y
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(f)
              be the unique map
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi Y
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X) \to S_\lambda(Y)
              such that
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi X
                              %mrow
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi λ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi X
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi f
                              %mrow
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mi n
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                            %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi λ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi Y
                              %mrow
                                %mo ⊗
                                %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mo →
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi S
                              %mi ν
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi Y
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    X^{\otimes n} &amp; \to &amp; S_\lambda(X) \\
                    f^{\otimes n} \downarrow \; \; &amp; &amp; \downarrow S_\lambda(f) \\
                    Y^{\otimes n} &amp; \to &amp; S_\nu(Y)
                    }
            %p commutes, where the horizontal arrows are the cokernel maps.
          %p
            More generally we can define a
            %strong Schur functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R : C \to C
          %p
            for any finite-dimensional representation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            as a finite direct sum of irreducible representations:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R = \bigoplus_i V_{\lambda_i}
          %p and then define
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                    %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi λ
                        %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R(-) = \bigoplus_i S_{\lambda_i}(-) \, .
          %h3#schur_functors_are_natural Schur functors are “natural”
          %p
            Suppose now that we have a symmetric monoidal linear functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            as a “change of base category”, and Schur functors are “natural” with respect to change of base.
          %p
            That is to say: if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            is a symmetric monoidal linear functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C \to D
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            preserves tensor products (at least up to coherent natural isomorphism), and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            will automatically preserve both direct sums (by linearity) as well as splittings of idempotents (as all functors do). Therefore, for a Schur functor
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi λ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi λ
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\lambda(X) = V_\lambda \otimes_{S_n} X^{\otimes n}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi D
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi G
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi D
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi G
                          %mi X
                          %msup
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi D
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi V
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi C
                          %msub
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi S
                                %mi n
                          %msup
                            %mi X
                            %mrow
                              %mo ⊗
                              %mi n
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≅
                        %mtd
                          %mi G
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mi λ
                              %mo ,
                              %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi X
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  S_{\lambda, D} (G X) = V_{\lambda, D} \otimes_{S_n} (G X)^{\otimes n} &amp; \cong &amp; V_{\lambda, D} \otimes_{S_n} G(X^{\otimes n}) \\
                  \&amp; \cong &amp; G(V_{\lambda, C}) \otimes_{S_n} G(X^{\otimes n}) \\
                  \&amp; \cong &amp; G(V_{\lambda, C} \otimes_{S_n} X^{\otimes n}) \\
                  \&amp; \cong &amp; G(S_{\lambda, C}(X))
                  }
          %p
            where the first isomorphism uses the symmetric monoidal structure of
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mrow
                      %mi λ
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_{\lambda, D} \cong G(V_{\lambda, C})
            because there is, up to isomorphism, only one symmetric monoidal linear functor
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi FinDimVect
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDimVect \to D
          %p
            If
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            is any representation, then by writing
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            as a direct sum of irreducible representations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi V
                    %mi λ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\lambda
            and using the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G
            preserves direct sums, we have more generally
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %mi D
                  %mo ∘
                  %mi G
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi G
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi R
                      %mo ,
                      %mi C
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_{R, D} \circ G \cong G \circ S_{R, C}.
          %p
            In summary, Schur functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            transfer “naturally” across change of base functors
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
          %h2#conceptual_description_of_schur_functors Conceptual description of Schur functors
          %p
            As we have seen, Schur functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            are definable under fairly mild hypotheses: working over a field of characteristic zero, they can be defined on any symmetric monoidal linear category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            which is Cauchy complete. So, for such
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            we can define a Schur functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R: C \to C
          %p
            and moreover, if
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
            is a symmetric monoidal linear functor, the Schur functors on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} D
            are “naturally” compatible, in the sense that the diagram
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %mover
                            %mo →
                            %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mi R
                              %mo ,
                              %mi C
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mrow
                              %mi R
                              %mo ,
                              %mi D
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mi C
                        %mtd
                          %munder
                            %mo →
                            %mi G
                        %mtd
                          %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\array{
                  C &amp; \stackrel{G}{\to} &amp; D \\
                  S_{R, C} \downarrow &amp; &amp; \downarrow S_{R, D} \\
                  C &amp; \underset{G}{\to} &amp; D
                  }
          %p
            commutes up to a canonical isomorphism
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ,
                        %mi D
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi G
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∘
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mrow
                        %mi R
                        %mo ,
                        %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_G: S_{R, D} \circ G \cong G \circ S_{R, C}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_G
            fit together sensibly when we compose symmetric monoidal linear functors.
          %p
            In this abstract framework, it may be wondered what significant role is played by the representations
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            of the symmetric group. The natural isomorphisms
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mi G
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_G
            which relate the Schur functors across change of base
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %mi C
                  %mo →
                  %mi D
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
            are pleasant to observe, but surely this is just some piddling general nonsense in the larger story of Schur functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
          %p
            Let us put the question another way. We have seen the Schur functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            are constructed in a uniform (or “polymorphic”) way across all symmetric monoidal Cauchy complete linear categories
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
            %em pseudonatural
            in the sense that naturality squares commute up to isomorphism
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi_G
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            from the class of all pseudonatural transformations
            = succeed "?" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
          %p
            The perhaps surprising answer is: no extra properties! That is, the Schur functors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_R
            are
            %em precisely
            those functors that are defined on all symmetric monoidal Cauchy complete linear
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            and that are pseudonatural with respect to change of base
            = succeed "!" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %mi C
                    %mo →
                    %mi D
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G: C \to D
          %p Let us now make this precise. Schur functors are defined on certain symmetric monoidal linear categories, but they respect neither the symmetric monoidal structure nor the linear structure. So, we have to forget some of the structure of the objects on which Schur functors are defined. This focuses our attention on the ‘forgetful’ 2-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SymMonLinCauch \to Cat
          %p where:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_2 Definition
            %p
              %strong SymMonLinCauch
              is the 2-category with
            %ul
              %li
                %p small symmetric monoidal Cauchy complete linear categories as objects,
              %li
                %p symmetric monoidal linear functors as morphisms,
              %li
                %p symmetric monoidal linear natural transformations as 2-morphisms.
          %p
            As we shall see, Schur functors correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pseudonatural+transformations"} pseudonatural transformations
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to itself, and morphisms between Schur functors correspond to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modifications"} modifications
            between these pseudonatural transformations. For the reader unaccustomed to these 2-categorical concepts, we recall:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_3 Definition
            %p
              Given two 2-functors
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ,
                    %mi V
                    %mo :
                    %mi S
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U, V: S \stackrel{\to}{\to} C
              between 2-categories, a
              %strong pseudonatural transformation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi: U \to V
              is a rule that assigns to each 0-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              a 1-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(s): U(s) \to V(s)
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi f
                    %mo :
                    %mi r
                    %mo →
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f: r \to s
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              an invertible 2-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi f
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi(f)
              of
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U(r) &amp; \stackrel{U(f)}{\to} &amp; U(s) \\
                    \\phi(r) \downarrow &amp; \phi(f) \swArrow &amp; \downarrow \phi(s) \\
                    V(r) &amp; \underset{V(f)}{\to} &amp; V(s)
                    }
            %p such that the following pasting diagram equalities hold:
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi g
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi g
                            %mi f
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi s
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi g
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi g
                                %mi f
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi t
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U(r) &amp; \stackrel{U(f)}{\to} &amp; U(s) &amp; \stackrel{U(g)}{\to} &amp; U(t) &amp; &amp; U(r) &amp; \stackrel{U(g f)}{\to} &amp; U(t) &amp; &amp; \\
                    \\phi(r) \downarrow &amp; \phi(f) \swArrow &amp; \downarrow \phi(s) &amp; \phi(g) \swArrow &amp; \downarrow \phi(t) &amp; = &amp; \phi(r) \downarrow &amp; \phi(g f) \swArrow &amp; \downarrow \phi(t) &amp; &amp; &amp; \\
                    V(r) &amp; \underset{V(f)}{\to} &amp; V(s) &amp; \underset{V(g)}{\to} &amp; V(t) &amp; &amp; V(r) &amp; \underset{V(g f)}{\to} &amp; V(t) &amp; &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p and
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mover
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi U
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi r
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                          %mtd
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %msub
                              %mn 1
                              %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ⇙
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ↓
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo =
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mn 1
                              %mrow
                                %mi ϕ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi r
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %munder
                              %mo →
                              %mrow
                                %mi V
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %msub
                                  %mn 1
                                  %mi r
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mi V
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi r
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    U(r) &amp; \stackrel{U(1_r)}{\to} &amp; U(r) &amp; &amp; \\
                    \\phi(r) \downarrow &amp; \phi(1_r) \swArrow &amp; \downarrow \phi(r) &amp; = &amp; 1_{\phi(r)} \\
                    V(r) &amp; \underset{V(1_r)}{\to} &amp; V(r) &amp; &amp;
                    }
            %p
              There is also a requirement that the assignation f \mapsto \phi(f) should be natural as f varies along a
              = succeed "-cell" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_4 Definition
            %p
              With notation as above, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo :
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi, \psi: U \to V
              be two pseudonatural transformations. A
              %strong modification
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo →
                    %mi ψ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x: \phi \to \psi
              is a rule which associates to each 0-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi S
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S
              a 2-cell
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi s
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi t
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x(s): \phi(s) \to \psi(t)
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
          %div{:style => "text-align:center"}
            %p
              %img{:alt => "tin can diagram for modification between psuedo natural transformations, drawn by John Baez", :src => "/nlab/files/tincan.png"}/
          %p We now propose our conceptual definition of Schur functor:
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_5 Definition
            %p
              An (abstract)
              %strong Schur functor
              is a pseudonatural transformation
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi S
                      %mo :
                      %mi U
                      %mo →
                      %mi U
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S: U \to U
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SymMonLinCauch \to Cat
            %p
              is the forgetful 2-functor. A
              %strong morphism
              of Schur functors is a modification between such pseudonatural transformations.
          %p
            What this proposed definition makes manifestly obvious is that
            = succeed "." do
              %em Schur functors are closed under composition
            = succeed "," do
              %em plethysm
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            defined earlier!
          %p
            Before launching into the proof, it is worth pondering an easier problem where we replace categories by sets, and symmetric monoidal linear Cauchy-complete categories by commutative rings.
            %br>/
            So, instead of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            let us consider
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonLinCauch
            let us consider
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi CommRing
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} CommRing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi CommRing
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U : CommRing \to Set \, .
          %p
            What are the natural transformations from this functor to itself? Any polynomial
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi P
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P \in \mathbb{Z}[x]
            defines such a natural transformation, since for any commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            there is a function
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_R: U(R) \to U(R)
            given by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi P
                    %mi R
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi P
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} P_R : x \mapsto P(x)
          %p
            and this is clearly natural in
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            %em precisely
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[x]
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[x]
            is the free commutative ring on one generator!
          %p To see this, note that the forgetful functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi CommRing
                  %mo →
                  %mi Set
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U : CommRing \to Set
          %p has a left adjoint, the ‘free commutative ring’ functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo :
                  %mi Set
                  %mo →
                  %mi CommRing
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F : Set \to CommRing \, .
          %p The free commutative ring on a 1-element set is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi ℤ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1) \cong \mathbb{Z}[x]
          %p
            and homomorphisms from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1)
            to any commutative ring
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi R
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
            are in one-to-one correspondence with elements of the underlying set of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi R
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} R
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi R
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(R) \cong hom(1, U(R)) \cong hom(F(1), R) \, .
          %p
            So, we say
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} represents
            the functor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            to itself is isomorphic to the underlying set of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℤ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %mi x
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{Z}[x]
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(F(1))
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi hom
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [U,U] \cong [hom(F(1), -), hom(F(1), -)] \cong hom(F(1), F(1)) \simeq U(F(1))
          %p
            In the first step here we use the representability
            = succeed ";" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \cong hom(F(1), -)
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yoneda+lemma"} Yoneda lemma
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
          %p
            We shall carry out a categorified version of this argument to prove that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            is the category of endomorphisms of the 2-functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi U
                  %mo :
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                  %mo →
                  %mi Cat
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SymMonLinCauch \to Cat
          %p
            The key is that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            is the free symmetric monoidal linear Cauchy-complete category on one generator.
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: I added explanatory remarks above. Okay?
          %h2#representability Representability
          %p
            To build a bridge from abstract Schur functors as pseudonatural transformations to the more classical descriptions, we start with the following key result. In what follows we use
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \mathbb{P}
            to denote the ‘linearization’ of the permutation groupoid: that is, the linear category formed by replacing the homsets in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            by the free vector spaces on those homsets. We use
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
            to denote the Cauchy completion of the linearization of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
            is equivalent to the category of Schur functors. But first:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem Theorem
            %p The underlying 2-functor
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SymMonLinCauch \to Cat
            %p
              is represented by
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mi ℙ
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(-) \simeq hom(-, \widebar{k \mathbb{P}})
          .proof
            %h6#proof_sketch Proof (Sketch)
            %p
              It is well-known that the permutation category
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℙ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo ≥
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \geq 0
              and whose morphisms are precisely automorphisms
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi m
                    %mo →
                    %mi m
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m \to m
              given by permutation groups
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi m
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_m
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 0
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonCat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo .
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0: SymMonCat \to Cat \, .
            %p
              Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymMonLin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonLin
              denote the 2-category of small symmetric monoidal linear (but not necessarily Cauchy complete) categories, and let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Lin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Lin
              denote the 2-category of small linear categories. Let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Lin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(-): Cat \to Lin
              denote linearization, given by change of base
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \cdot - : Set \to Vect
            %p
              applied to a
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ,
                    %mi hom
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo ×
                    %msub
                      %mi C
                      %mn 0
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_0, hom: C_0 \times C_0 \to Set)
              to yield a linear category
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ,
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi C
                        %mn 0
                      %mo →
                      %mi Vect
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (C_0, C_0 \times C_0 \to Vect)
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi Cat
                    %mo →
                    %mi Lin
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(-): Cat \to Lin
              is left 2-adjoint to the underlying 2-functor
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi U
                        %mn 0
                      %mo :
                      %mi Lin
                      %mo →
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U_0: Lin \to Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              is a nice closed category (here
              = succeed ")" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Vect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi I
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi V
                    %mo →
                    %mi Set
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \hom(I, -): V \to Set
              has a left adjoint
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⋅
                    %mi I
                    %mo :
                    %mi Set
                    %mo →
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \cdot I: Set \to V
              (here linearization) which is strong (symmetric) monoidal. This induces a 2-functor
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo =
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat = Set
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                      %mo →
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat \to V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              which is strong 2-symmetric monoidal. It therefore sends symmetric pseudomonoids in
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Set
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Set
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              to symmetric pseudomonoids in
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k(-) \dashv U_0
              between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              and
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinCat
              lifts to one between
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymMonCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonCat
              and
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi SymMonLinCat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonLinCat
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonCat
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymMonLin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 1
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLin
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymMonCat
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (k(-): SymMonCat \to SymMonLin) \dashv (U_1: SymMonLin \to SymMonCat)
            %p
              Finally, let
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi LinCauch
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} LinCauch
              denote the 2-category of small Cauchy complete linear categories. The linear Cauchy completion gives a 2-reflector
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi Lin
                    %mo →
                    %mi LinCat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{(-)}: Lin \to LinCat
              which is left 2-adjoint to the 2-embedding
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mo :
                      %mi LinCauch
                      %mo →
                      %mi Lin
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i: LinCauch \to Lin
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{(-)} \dashv i
              lifts to the level of symmetric monoidal structure to give a 2-adjunction
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLin
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊣
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi U
                      %mn 2
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mo →
                    %mi SymMonLin
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\widebar{(-)}: SymMonLin \to SymMonLinCauch) \dashv (U_2: SymMonLinCauch \to SymMonLin)
            %p
              For this, the key fact is that if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A \otimes B
              denotes the tensor product of two
              = succeed "-enriched" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mi A
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mover
                      %mi B
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ≃
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi A
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi B
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{A} \otimes \overline{B} \simeq \overline{A \otimes B}
              making Cauchy completion into a lax 2-monoidal functor on
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
            %p
              Putting this all together, the underlying functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo :
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mo →
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U: SymMonLinCauch \to Cat
              is the evident composite
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi SymMonLinCauch
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 2
                    %mi SymMonLin
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 1
                    %mi SymMonCat
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi U
                          %mn 0
                    %mi Cat
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonLinCauch \stackrel{U_2}{\to} SymMonLin \stackrel{U_1}{\to} SymMonCat \stackrel{U_0}{\to} Cat
            %p and therefore we have pseudonatural equivalences
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi SymMonLinCauch
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mover
                              %mrow
                                %mi k
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                                %mi ℙ
                                %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                              %mo ¯
                            %mo ,
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                          %mtd
                            %mi SymMonLin
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi k
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℙ
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 2
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                          %mtd
                            %mi SymMonCat
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                            %mi ℙ
                            %mo ,
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 2
                            %mo −
                            %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 0
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 1
                            %msub
                              %mi U
                              %mn 2
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                          %mtd
                            %mo ≅
                          %mtd
                            %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                    \\array{
                    SymMonLinCauch(\widebar{k(\mathbb{P})}, -) &amp; \cong &amp; SymMonLin(k(\mathbb{P}), U_2 -) \\
                    \&amp; \cong &amp; SymMonCat(\mathbb{P}, U_1 U_2 -) \\
                    \&amp; \cong &amp; U_0 U_1 U_2 \\
                    \&amp; \cong &amp; U
                    }
            %p
              so that
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
              is the representing object.
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: I took remarks from the query box here and used them to improve the proof above. However, it could still use more improvement. Could you polish it up a bit, Todd?
          %h3#structure_of_the_representing_object Structure of the representing object
          %p
            Let us now calculate
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            consists of the full subcategory of linear presheaves
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi C
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C^{op} \to Vect
            that are obtained as retracts of finite direct sums of representables
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi c
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi C
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C(-, c): C^{op} \to Vect
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                    %mo =
                    %mi k
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C = k\mathbb{P}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi FinDimVect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \colon \mathbb{P}^{op} \to FinDimVect
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n) = 0
            for large enough
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            is a sum of monomials
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 0
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi ⋯
                    %mo ⊕
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(0) \oplus F(1) \oplus \cdots \oplus F(n)
            = succeed "-module" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi j
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_j
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(j)
            is the retract of a finite sum of copies of the group algebra
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_j]
            which corresponds to the representable
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ,
                    %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\mathbb{P}(-, j)
          %p
            So, inspired by Joyal’s work on
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/combinatorial+species"} combinatorial species
          .num_defn
            %h6#definition_6 Definition
            %p
              A
              %strong polynomial species
              is a functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi FinDimVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: \mathbb{P}^{op} \to FinDimVect
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo =
                    %mn 0
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(n) = 0
              for all sufficiently large
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n
          %p
            As we have mentioned, the category of polynomial species inherits two monoidal structures from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
            via
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            %em additive
            monoidal structure on
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi m
                  %mo ×
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo →
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_m \times S_n \to S_{m+n}
          %p which juxtapose permutations. This can be linearized to give algebra maps
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi m
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo →
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mrow
                      %mi m
                      %mo +
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_m] \otimes k[S_n] \to k[S_{m+n}]
          %p
            which give the monoidal category structure of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\mathbb{P}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Day+convolution"} Day convolution
            to the Cauchy completion
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k\mathbb{P}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi k
                  %mi ℙ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\mathbb{P}
            and the category of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mi ℙ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k\mathbb{P}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo ,
                  %mi G
                  %mo :
                  %msup
                    %mi ℙ
                    %mi op
                  %mo →
                  %mi Vect
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G: \mathbb{P}^{op} \to Vect
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi j
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi k
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F G)(n) = \sum_{j+k = n} (F(j) \otimes G(k)) \otimes_{S_j \times S_k} k[S_n]
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              \: Todd had written
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
              where I have put
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              here. Okay?
          %p or, in other notation,
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Ind
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi j
                      %mo ×
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F G)(n) = \sum_{j+k = n} Ind_{S_j \times S_k}^{S_n} F(j) \otimes G(k)
          %p and by restriction this formula gives a tensor product on polynomial species. This tensor product is a kind of categorification of the usual definition of product of ordinary polynomials, where given
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi j
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi M
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi f
                        %mi j
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi j
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo !
                  %mspace{:width => "2em"}
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mn 0
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≤
                      %mi N
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi g
                        %mi k
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mi k
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo !
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x) = \sum_{0 \leq j \leq M} \frac{f_j x^j}{j!} \qquad G(x) = \sum_{0 \leq k \leq N} \frac{g_k x^k}{k!}
          %p
            the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi n
                    %mi th
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n^{th}
            Taylor coefficient of the product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F(x)G(x)
            is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo +
                      %mi k
                      %mo =
                      %mi n
                  %mfrac
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo !
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mo !
                      %mi k
                      %mo !
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mi j
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mi k
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sum_{j+k = n} \frac{n!}{j! k!} f_j g_k
          %p So in summary:
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_2 Theorem
            %p
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
              is equivalent to the symmetric monoidal category of polynomial species.
          %p
            Now, having defined Schur functors abstractly as pseudonatural transformations
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [U, U] \cong SymMonLinCauch(\widebar{k\mathbb{P}}, \widebar{k\mathbb{P}}) \cong U(\widebar{k\mathbb{P}})
          %p In other words,
          .num_theorem
            %h6#theorem_3 Theorem
            %p
              The category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Schur
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
              is equivalent to the category of polynomial species
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi ℙ
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi FinDimVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^{op} \to FinDimVect
          %p
            NB: This theorem refers only to the underlying category
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U(\overline{k\mathbb{P}})
          %h2#composition_of_schur_functors Composition of Schur functors
          %p
            Now we consider composition of Schur functors
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi U
                    %mo →
                    %mi U
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to U
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k\mathbb{P}} \to \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi U
                  %mo ,
                  %mi U
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [U, U]
            with a monoidal structure, and this monoidal structure transfers across the equivalence of the preceding theorem to a monoidal structure on the underlying category of Schur functors, or equivalently, polynomial species
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi FinDimVect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}^{op} \to FinDimVect
          %p It may be easier to do this in reverse. Any Schur functor may regarded as a functor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mi F
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1 \stackrel{F}{\to} \overline{k\mathbb{P}}  \, .
          %p This induces a symmetric monoidal functor, unique up to (unique) symmetric monoidal isomorphism:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ℙ
                  %mover
                    %mo →
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi F
                        %mo ∼
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo :
                  %mi m
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P} \stackrel{F^\sim}{\to}\overline{k\mathbb{P}}: m \mapsto F^{\otimes m}
          %p
            Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^{\otimes m}
            is a Day convolution product of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi m
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} m
            copies of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F^\sim
            is linearized and extended (uniquely) to the linear Cauchy completion, to give a symmetric monoidal linear functor on
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \widebar{k \mathbb{P}}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℙ
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∼
                  %mo :
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo →
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \otimes_{\mathbb{P}} F^\sim: \overline{k\mathbb{P}} \to \overline{k\mathbb{P}}
          %p
            as this manifestly preserves colimits in the blank argument and therefore all colimits needed for the Cauchy completion. (And since the extension to the Cauchy completion is unique, this formula must be correct! The only question is whether this functor is valued in
            = succeed ".)" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{k\mathbb{P}}
          %p
            In the language of species, this construction is called the
            = succeed "," do
              %em substitution product
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ∘
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \circ F
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            in ways that might be confusing, we will rename it
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi G
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \boxtimes F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi F
                  %mo =
                  %mi G
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi ℙ
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mo ∼
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \boxtimes F = G \otimes_{\mathbb{P}} F^\sim
          %p In notation which looks slightly less abstract, this is the Schur object given by the formula
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo ≥
                      %mn 0
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %msub
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi k
                  %msup
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mi k
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G \boxtimes F)(n) = \sum_{k \geq 0} G(k) \otimes_{S_k} F^{\otimes k}(n)
          %p
            It should be noted that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi n
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (G \boxtimes F)(n)
            is indeed
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 0
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 0
            for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi n
                    %mo &gt;
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi deg
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi deg
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} n \gt (deg G)(deg F)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi G
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} G \boxtimes F
            is indeed a polynomial species. It is just the polynomial special case of the substitution product which is defined on general
            %strong linear species
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ,
                    %mi G
                    %mo :
                    %msup
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mi op
                    %mo →
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F, G: \mathbb{P}^{op} \to Vect
          .num_prop
            %h6#proposition_2 Proposition
            %p
              The product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo ⊠
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxtimes
              makes the category of polynomial species into a monoidal category. The unit for this product is polynomial species
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              given by the representable
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℙ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo :
                      %msup
                        %mi ℙ
                        %mi op
                      %mo →
                      %mi FinDimVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{P}(-, 1): \mathbb{P}^{op} \to FinDimVect
          .proof
            %h6#proof_sketch_2 Proof (Sketch)
            %p
              The following proof is adapted from a similar argument due to Max Kelly [ref]: we exhibit an associativity isomorphism
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi α
                    %mo :
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: (- \boxtimes F) \boxtimes G \to - \boxtimes (F \boxtimes G)
              on the basis of universal properties. The point is that by the universal property of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mi ℙ
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{k\mathbb{P}}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo :
                    %mn 1
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F: 1 \to \overline{k\mathbb{P}}
            %p is equivalent to the category of symmetric monoidal linear functors
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                    %mo :
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo →
                    %mover
                      %mrow
                        %mi k
                        %mi ℙ
                      %mo ¯
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H: \overline{k\mathbb{P}} \to \overline{k\mathbb{P}}
            %p
              The correspondence in one direction takes
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              to the symmetric monoidal functor
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi H
                      %mo =
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H = - \boxtimes F
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi H
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} H
              to
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo =
                      %mi H
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F = H(X)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi F
                      %mo ≅
                      %mi F
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X \boxtimes F \cong F
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi G
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi G
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \boxtimes F) \boxtimes G \to - \boxtimes (F \boxtimes G)
            %p
              are in natural bijection with morphisms
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi X
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X \boxtimes F) \boxtimes G \to X \boxtimes (F \boxtimes G)
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \boxtimes G \to F \boxtimes G
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi G
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F \boxtimes G
              we obtain an associativity map
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mo :
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                      %mo →
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi F
                      %mo ⊠
                      %mi G
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha: (- \boxtimes F) \boxtimes G \to - \boxtimes (F \boxtimes G)
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mrow
                          %mi k
                          %mi ℙ
                        %mo ¯
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{k \mathbb{P}}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha
          %p To summarize: we have equivalences between
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                The category of pseudonatural transformations
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi U
                        %mo →
                        %mi U
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} U \to U
            %li
              %p
                The category of symmetric monoidal linear functors
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mi ℙ
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo →
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mi ℙ
                          %mo ¯
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{k\mathbb{P}} \to \overline{k \mathbb{P}}
            %li
              %p
                The category
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi Schur
                        %mo =
                        %mi U
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %mover
                          %mrow
                            %mi k
                            %mi ℙ
                          %mo ¯
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur = U(\overline{k\mathbb{P}})
          %p
            The equivalence
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                  %mo →
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur \to SymMonLinCauch(\overline{k \mathbb{P}}, \overline{k \mathbb{P}})
            takes a polynomial species
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo ⊠
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} - \boxtimes F
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi F
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi F
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mi G
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (- \boxtimes G) \circ (- \boxtimes F) \to - \boxtimes (F \boxtimes G)
          %p
            of strong monoidal equivalence from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi Schur
                  %mo ,
                  %mo ⊠
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (Schur, \boxtimes)
            to the monoidal category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi SymMonLinCauch
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo ,
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mi ℙ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} SymMonLinCauch(\overline{k\mathbb{P}}, \overline{k\mathbb{P}})
            under endofunctor composition. (The hexagonal coherence condition for a monoidal functor follows from the pentagon; one side of the hexagon is an identity since endofunctor composition is a strict monoidal product.)
          %p
            The tensor product
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊠
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \boxtimes
            on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Schur
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Schur
            goes by another name: it is the
            = succeed "." do
              %strong plethystic tensor product
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/plethory"} plethory
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Specht+module"} Specht module
          %h2#references References
          %p A nice introduction to Schur functors can be found here:
          %ul
            %li
              William Fulton and Joe Harris,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Representation Theory: a First Course
          %p
            For a quick online introduction to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Young+tableaux"} Young tableaux
            and representations of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+groups"} symmetric groups
          %ul
            %li
              Yufei Zhao, Young tableaux and the representations of the symmetric group, MIT. (
              %a{:href => "http://www.thehcmr.org/issue2_2/tableaux.pdf"}> web
              )
          %p For more details on these topics, see:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                William Fulton,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Young Tableaux, with Applications to Representation Theory and Geometry
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Bruce+E.+Sagan"}> Bruce E. Sagan
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Symmetric Group: Representations, Combinatorial Algorithms, and Symmetric Functions
          %p A treatment of the category of Schur functors as the free 2-rig on one generator is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Joe+Moeller"} Joe Moeller
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Todd+Trimble"} Todd Trimble
              = succeed "," do
                %em Schur functors and categorified plethysm
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.00190"} arXiv:2106.00190
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/species"} Species
            were invented here:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                , Une théorie combinatoire des séries formelles,
                %em Adv. Math
                %strong 42
                (1981), 1–82.
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andr%C3%A9+Joyal"}> André Joyal
                \: Foncteurs analytiques et espèces des structures, in
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Combinatoire Énumérative
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 1234
          %p A standard reference on species is:
          %ul
            %li
              François Bergeron, Gilbert Labelle, Pierre Leroux,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Combinatorial Species and Tree-like Structures
          %p
            Here is an important new book on combinatorics which emphasizes the use of
            = succeed "-valued" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Vect
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Vect
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marcelo+Aguiar"} Marcelo Aguiar
              and
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Swapneel+Mahajan"} Swapneel Mahajan
              = succeed "," do
                %em Monoidal Functors, Species and Hopf Algebras
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.tamu.edu/~maguiar/a.pdf"} web
          %p Characteristic-free approach to Schur functors and the notion of Schur complexes are developed in
          %ul
            %li
              Kaan Akin,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Alvin+Buchsbaum"} David Alvin Buchsbaum
              = succeed "," do
                %em Schur functors and Schur complexes
              = succeed ":3" do
                %strong 44
            %li
              Marcin Chałupnik,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Extensions of Weyl and Schur functors
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Brandenburg"}> Martin Brandenburg
              ,
              %em Operations on categories of modules are given by Schur functors
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1610.02180"}> arXiv:1610.02180
              )
          %h2#old_stuff Old Stuff
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
            \: this stuff should go somewhere, perhaps:
          .query
            %p
              The first thing that should be understood from the beginning is that a general Schur functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              = succeed ":" do
                %em nonlinear
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom-set"} hom-sets
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo ,
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo →
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi V
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mi F
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi W
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(V, W) \to hom(F(V), F(W))
            %p
              is not assumed to respect the linear structure. In fact, linear Schur functors are rather uninteresting: because every finite-dimensional space is a finite
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/direct+sum"} direct sum
              of copies of the
              = succeed "-dimensional" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mn 1
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ℂ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+functor"} linear functors
              preserve finite direct sums (that is,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/biproduct"} biproducts
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi F
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
              is
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/representable+functor"} representable
              as
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi hom
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi X
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} hom(X, -)
              where
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi X
                      %mo =
                      %mi F
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi ℂ
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X = F(\mathbb{C})
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi FinDimVect
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} FinDimVect
          %p
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/John+Baez"}> John Baez
            \: This stuff should get worked into the discussion near the end of how Schur functors are like polynomials…
          .query
            %h3#modules_over_a_bimonoid Modules over a bimonoid
            %p
              Next we exploit the fact that, just like any
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group+algebra"} group algebra
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              is a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bialgebra"} bialgebra
              — or in fancier language, a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimonoid"} bimonoid
              in the
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/FinDimVect"} FinDimVect
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mrow
                        %mi k
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} {}_k
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                    %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                    %msub
                      %mi FinDimVect
                      %mi k
                    %mo →
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i \colon FinDimVect_k \to C
              is a
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+functor"} symmetric monoidal functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi i
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} i
              carries
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              to a bimonoid in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
            %p
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category+of+modules"} category of modules
              over a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/bimonoid"} bimonoid
              is a
              = succeed "." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              in
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              with comultiplication
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi δ
                    %mo :
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo →
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                    %mo ,
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \delta: k[S_n] \to k[S_n] \otimes k[S_n] \,,
            %p
              the tensor product
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V \otimes W
              of two
              = succeed "-modules" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi k
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              carries a module structure where the action is defined by
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi δ
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mn 1
                          %mrow
                            %mi V
                            %mo ⊗
                            %mi W
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mn 1
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mn 1
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi V
                        %mo ⊗
                        %msub
                          %mi α
                          %mi W
                    %mi V
                    %mo ⊗
                    %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n] \otimes V \otimes W \stackrel{\delta \otimes 1_{V \otimes W}}{\to} k[S_n] \otimes k[S_n] \otimes V \otimes W \stackrel{1 \otimes \sigma \otimes 1}{\to} k[S_n] \otimes V \otimes k[S_n] \otimes W \stackrel{\alpha_V \otimes \alpha_W}{\to} V \otimes W
            %p
              where
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
              is a symmetry isomorphism and
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mi V
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_V
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi α
                      %mi W
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \alpha_W
              are the actions on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi V
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V
              and
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi W
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} W
            %p
              Now we consider a particular case of tensor product representations. If
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
              is an object of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
              has a representation on
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n}
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S_n
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                      %mo →
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo ≅
                    %munder
                      %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ⨁
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ∈
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                    %mi I
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n] \otimes X^{\otimes n} \cong \bigoplus_{\sigma \in S_n} I \otimes X^{\otimes n} \to X^{\otimes n}
            %p
              which is the required representation.) So, if
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\nu
              is a Young tableau representation of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              in
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n}
            %p
              of
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k[S_n]
              in
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            %p
              Consider next the
              %strong averaging operator
              = succeed ":" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi e
                      %mo =
                      %mfrac
                        %mn 1
                        %mrow
                          %mi n
                          %mo !
                      %msub
                        %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∑
                        %mrow
                          %mi σ
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi S
                            %mi n
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = \frac1{n!} \sum_{\sigma \in S_n} \sigma
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo :
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e: V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n} \to V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n}
            %p
              This operator makes sense since
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k
              has characteristic zero, and crucially, this operator is
              %em idempotent
              (because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mi σ
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = \sigma e
              for all
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                      %mo ∈
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S_n
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi C
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mover
                      %munder
                        %mo →
                        %mn 1
                      %mover
                        %mo →
                        %mi e
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mo →
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %msub
                      %mo ⊗
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi n
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n} \stackrel{\overset{e}{\to}}{\underset{1}{\to}} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n} \to V_\nu \otimes_{S_n} X^{\otimes n}
            %p
              This
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coequalizer"} coequalizer
              is indeed the object of
              = succeed "-" do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi S
                        %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_n
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/coinvariants"} coinvariants
              of
              = succeed "," do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi V
                        %mi ν
                      %mo ⊗
                      %msup
                        %mi X
                        %mrow
                          %mo ⊗
                          %mi n
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n}
            .maruku-equation
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                    %mover
                      %mo →
                      %mrow
                        %mi σ
                        %mo ⋅
                        %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                    %msub
                      %mi V
                      %mi ν
                    %mo ⊗
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mo ⊗
                        %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n} \stackrel{\sigma \cdot-}{\to} V_\nu \otimes X^{\otimes n}
            %p
              ranging over
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi σ
                    %mo ∈
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi n
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma \in S_n
              (it is the joint coequalizer because
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi e
                    %mo =
                    %mi σ
                    %mi e
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} e = \sigma e
              for all
              = succeed ")." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi σ
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \sigma
            %p
              We may now define the Schur functor
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msub
                      %mi S
                      %mi ν
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_\nu
              on
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi C
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
              attached to a Young tableau
              = succeed "." do
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi ν
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \nu
